! Lab 04: Routing Tables and Static Routes
! Platform: Cisco Packet Tracer
! Devices: R1, ISP, SW1
! Purpose:
! - Understand how routers decide where to send traffic
! - Read connected, local, static and default routes
! - Understand next-hop IP addresses
! - Understand return routes
! - Understand why ping can fail one way
! - Make show ip route feel normal before moving to STP, OSPF and wireless

! Go to R1
show ip route

Gateway of last resort is 203.0.113.1 to network 0.0.0.0

     192.168.10.0/24 is variably subnetted, 2 subnets, 2 masks
C    192.168.10.0/24 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0.10
L    192.168.10.1/32 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0.10

     192.168.20.0/24 is variably subnetted, 2 subnets, 2 masks
C    192.168.20.0/24 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0.20
L    192.168.20.1/32 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0.20

     192.168.30.0/24 is variably subnetted, 2 subnets, 2 masks
C    192.168.30.0/24 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0.30
L    192.168.30.1/32 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0.30

     192.168.99.0/24 is variably subnetted, 2 subnets, 2 masks
C    192.168.99.0/24 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0.99
L    192.168.99.1/32 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0.99

     203.0.113.0/30 is variably subnetted, 2 subnets, 2 masks
C    203.0.113.0/30 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/1
L    203.0.113.2/32 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/1

S*   0.0.0.0/0 [1/0] via 203.0.113.1

! local route example:
! L 192.168.10.1/32 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0.10
! L means local
! Local routes are the router's own interface IP addresses
! /32 means one exact host address
! 192.168.10.1 is R1's own IP address on VLAN 10

! default route example:
! S* 0.0.0.0/0 [1/0] via 203.0.113.1
! S means static
! * means default route

! If R1 does not know a destination network it sends traffic to 203.0.113.1
! 203.0.113.1 is the next-hop IP

! Go to ISP

show ip route

! ISP only knows directly connected networks

! Connected networks:
! 198.51.100.0/24
! 203.0.113.0/30

! ISP does not know the internal VLAN networks:
! 192.168.10.0/24
! 192.168.20.0/24
! 192.168.30.0/24
! 192.168.99.0/24

! Ping requires a return path
! Reply traffic must know how to get back

! Test from Internet-PC

ping 192.168.10.10

! Result: unreachable
! Reason:
! ISP does not know how to reach 192.168.10.0/24
! Routers can only forward traffic to networks they know about

! Go to ISP

enable
configure terminal

ip route 192.168.10.0 255.255.255.0 203.0.113.2

end

! Verify static route

show ip route

Gateway of last resort is not set

S    192.168.10.0/24 [1/0] via 203.0.113.2

     198.51.100.0/24 is variably subnetted, 2 subnets, 2 masks
C    198.51.100.0/24 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/1
L    198.51.100.1/32 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/1

     203.0.113.0/24 is variably subnetted, 2 subnets, 2 masks
C    203.0.113.0/30 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0
L    203.0.113.1/32 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0

! S means static route
! ISP now knows:
! 192.168.10.0/24 is reachable through R1
! via 203.0.113.2 means:
! send traffic to R1

! Note:
! This lab demonstrates routing behaviour and return paths
! In real environments inbound access would usually be controlled with ACLs or firewalls

! Add static routes on ISP for internal VLAN networks
! Go to ISP

configure terminal

ip route 192.168.20.0 255.255.255.0 203.0.113.2
ip route 192.168.30.0 255.255.255.0 203.0.113.2
ip route 192.168.99.0 255.255.255.0 203.0.113.2

end

show ip route

Gateway of last resort is not set

S    192.168.10.0/24 [1/0] via 203.0.113.2
S    192.168.20.0/24 [1/0] via 203.0.113.2
S    192.168.30.0/24 [1/0] via 203.0.113.2
S    192.168.99.0/24 [1/0] via 203.0.113.2

! ISP now knows all internal VLAN networks
! All traffic for internal VLANs is sent to R1

! Test connectivity from Internet-PC

ping 192.168.20.10
ping 192.168.30.10
ping 192.168.99.2

! Result:
! successful replies

! Static routes on ISP now allow return traffic
! ISP knows to send internal VLAN traffic to R1

! Go to R1
write memory

! Go to ISP
write memory